S2S clients find peers by `service_id` alone. Nothing in the discovery, cert or token checks looks at location, cluster or labels. Line numbers are in the template files under `/builds/looper/tacplus/issues/204/pkg/templates/`, except proto and `pkg/fleet` paths. I read Go in full and only checked parity points in Java and Rust.

## 1. Peer discovery
- **Source.** Health comes from the `ServiceHealth` state root (`pkg/proto/sys/servicehealth.proto:17-27`). It is `service_state {label:["kernel"]}`, keyed `service_id@instance_id`, and entries expire by TTL. Each `ServiceInstanceHealth` (`pkg/proto/sys/healthreport.proto:27-73`) carries `service_id` ("from token sub"), `grpc_endpoint`, `labels` (field 18) and `spiffe_id` (field 21).
- **Go wiring.** The S2S path never builds generated client wrappers itself; it uses the service-manager wiring in `go/types/service_manager.go.tmpl:484-522`:
  - It makes one `serviceconnection.NewDynamic(svc, sc.ServiceDialOpts(svc))` per target.
  - It registers `ServiceHealth().OnInstancesChange`, groups instances by `inst.ServiceId` only (`:507-511`), then calls `ReconcileFromHealth`.
  - Tenant-routed targets are grouped again by the tenant parsed from `spiffe_id` (`go/chassis/serviceconnection/serviceconnection.go.tmpl:460-463`).
- **Reconcile.** `ReconcileFromHealth` (`serviceconnection.go.tmpl:260-327`) keys on `host:port` from `GrpcEndpoint`. It skips UNHEALTHY instances when dialing, and closes an endpoint only after 6 snapshots in a row omit it (`:48`). It routes to HEALTHY or DEGRADED endpoints (`:129-133`).
- **Java.** Polls `getAllInstances` and filters with `"{{.ServiceID}}".equals(inst.getServiceId())` (`java/types/ServiceManager.java.tmpl:1411-1426`).
- **Rust.** Filters with `.filter(|i| i.service_id == sc.service_id())` (`rust/types/service_manager.rs.tmpl:2024`).
- **No cluster scoping.** No filter uses `labels`, `spiffe_id` location or trust domain, or cluster. Peers are scoped only by whatever instances report into the one kernel-hosted `ServiceHealth`, reached through the bootstrap `cfg.Endpoints`.

## 2. Client authentication
- **Token.** `smgrpc.New(s2sChannels, aud => identity.GetTokenFor(aud))` (`service_manager.go.tmpl:526-528`). Each client gets `tokenProviderFor("{{.TargetServiceID}}")` (`go/types/grpcclient_group.go.tmpl:39`), so the audience is the target's service ID (the hosting service, for virtual services). The token is sent as `Authorization: Bearer` (`go/types/grpcclient.go.tmpl:72-86`).
- **Minting** (`go/chassis/service_im/service_im.go.tmpl`):
  - `GetTokenFor` is at `:466`.
  - Phase 1 exchanges the k8s or bootstrap token for refresh and access tokens.
  - Phase 2 calls `MintServiceToken{InputToken, Aud: audience, TokenPurpose: SERVICE}` (`:879-886`).
  - Tokens are cached per audience.
- **Server cert check.**
  - `ServiceDialOpts(serviceID)` → `VerifyPeerServiceIdentity(cert, serviceID, bc.trustDomain)` (`go/types/servicemanager_lib.go.tmpl:495-502`).
  - `spiffeTLSDialOpt` (`:459-480`) sets `InsecureSkipVerify` and does its own chain check against `certPool`. That pool is built from `cfg.TrustedCas` (`:289-314`), a single bundle with fail-closed if empty, and `ErrMissingTrustDomain` if `spiffe_trust_domain` is empty.
  - The SAN check (`go/chassis/x509/x509.go.tmpl:205-228`) needs a URI SAN that parses as `KindService`, with `TrustDomain == configured TD` and `SubjectPath == serviceName`. Any location segments are accepted.
  - The bootstrap or state connection pins only the trust domain (`VerifyPeerInTrustDomain`, `x509.go.tmpl:250-271`; `servicemanager_lib.go.tmpl:321-323`; `service_im.go.tmpl:222-267`).
  - Rust uses `spiffe_verifier_base.clone().expect_service(id)` (`service_manager.rs.tmpl:805-816`). Its comment says the SAN is `spiffe://<td>/<service_id>`, which is out of date.

## 3. Server-side admission
All in `go/chassis/grpcserver/authorizer.go.tmpl`.
- **`verifyBearer`** (`:702-735`):
  - Calls `verifyToken`, which looks up the token's `kid` in `JwkCache` (`:955-972`).
  - Rejects the API token class.
  - Requires `aud ∈ acceptedAudiences`: own label, hosted traits, and `accepted_audiences` (`:202-212`).
  - Applies the host-tenant check.
- **`Authorize` S2S path** (`:527-562`): when `sub_type` is service or workload, `tw.ServicePath()` matches against the per-method `auth_services` RE2 patterns.
  - `ServicePath` (`go/chassis/token/token.go.tmpl:422-442`) uses `ServicePathFromSpiffe(sub)` (`go/chassis/spiffe/spiffe.go.tmpl:639-652`). It drops the trust domain and `/system/<location>`: "same service matches identically across clouds/accounts/AZs/clusters… (Location-scoped authz is deferred; see plan D2.)"
- **Issuer and trust domain on tokens are not pinned.** `Verify` (`token.go.tmpl:980-1000+`) checks only that `iss` is non-empty, plus kid, alg, exp and nbf. The authorizer compares neither the token `sub` trust domain nor `iss` to config.
- **Which keys verify tokens.** The authorizer's `JwkCache` is fed by `JwkVerifyingKeysViewService.Watch` on the bootstrap connection (`:250-255`, `:142-180`). That service is an aggregate of the cluster's `view_member "JwkVerifyingKeysView"` collections (`pkg/proto/sys/jwkview.proto:7-29`). `ClusterService.WatchJwks` is a separate stream that fills `bc.jwkCache` (`servicemanager_lib.go.tmpl:1014-1090`). Its comment says kernel does not implement it (UNIMPLEMENTED).
- **"Pins the bootstrap trust domain."** This is TLS only: `service_im.go.tmpl:222-267` pins the token authority's cert to the trust domain.
- **`auth_services_from_fleet`** (`pkg/proto/sys/spec.proto:1284-1290`). `pkg/fleet/auth.go:22-91` (`stampFleetAuth`, called from `pkg/fleet/fleet.go:380-384`) rewrites the merged protos so each such service gets the set of fleet services declaring a `grpc_s2s_client` of it, or `all_grpc_s2s_clients`. If there are no callers it stays fail-closed. This happens at build time, by name, with no cluster dimension.

## 4. Trust domain, location and SPIFFE helpers
- `Config.spiffe_trust_domain` (16) and `location_path` (17) are bootstrap/runtime config. `location_path` is "Supplied by the operator/deployment… not a token claim" (`pkg/proto/sys/grpcconfig.proto:142-156`).
- The own SPIFFE ID is built as `ServiceSpiffeID(cfg.SpiffeTrustDomain, cfg.LocationPath, serviceAudience)` (`servicemanager_lib.go.tmpl:573`) for the X509Mint request.
- Helpers in `go/chassis/spiffe/spiffe.go.tmpl`: `ServiceSpiffeID` (:279), `ParseSpiffeID` (:447) with `LocationPath` (:432, :558), `ServicePathFromSpiffe` (:643), `SameTrustDomain` (:728), `NormalizeTrustDomain` (:116). Python and TypeScript have matching versions.

## 5. Multi-cluster, federation and external issuers
- `ClusterInfo` and `ClusterState` ("multi-region, multi-cluster topology") plus `ClusterService.Subscribe` (`grpcconfig.proto:265-308`). This feed is only logged (`servicemanager_lib.go.tmpl:1003`) and does not affect S2S routing.
- `MintExternalServiceToken` (`pkg/proto/sys/minter.proto:39-44`) and `IdentityManager.TokenForExternal` (`service_im.go.tmpl:497-531`) are outbound only. They mint a kernel-signed token for an off-cluster audience (workload identity federation), uncached, at most 600s. Java and Rust have matching versions.
- `PublicJwksViewService` (`pkg/proto/sys/publicjwksview.proto:7-35`) is read only by the gateway to publish `/.well-known/jwks.json`. It includes the external (WIF ES256) signing keys and is not used to verify incoming tokens.
- Java `MemberPort` mentions "fleet trust bundle", meaning the same `trustedCas` (`java/chassis/memberport/MemberPort.java.tmpl:77,231`).
- I found no multiple trust bundles, cross-cluster discovery, external-issuer verification, or per-cluster `ServiceHealth`. Location-scoped authorization is explicitly deferred ("plan D2"); the only references are code comments.
