< 0 https://github.com/tacplus-dev/tacplus/issues/204#issuecomment-6694736264 https://github.com/tacplus-dev/tacplus/issues/204 ["needs-decision","approved","looper:in-progress","feature"]
Nothing was built or committed. The issue requires Jody to approve the design on the issue before any building starts, and no design had been posted. So I posted one: https://github.com/tacplus-dev/tacplus/issues/204#issuecomment-6694736264. I also added the `needs-decision` label and stopped, as rule 1 says.

**How it works today:**
- **Discovery:** a generated S2S client finds peers by service name alone, from the one health record its own cluster's kernel keeps.
- **Trust:** it checks peers against one certificate bundle and one key set.
- **Admission:** the server doesn't check the token's issuer or the trust domain in its subject. That's safe while only the local kernel's keys are trusted, but any cross-site trust has to add those checks.

**What I propose (D1–D8):**
- **D1, declaring it:**
  - On the caller: `(spec.grpc_s2s_client) = {service, remote: true}`, which generates a remote client group chosen by site name.
  - On the server: a new `(spec.service_s2s).remote_auth_services` list, empty by default, that admits named services only.
  - The tenant door and route checks stay as they are.
  - Generation fails if a remote client's target doesn't admit it.
- **D2, finding the peer:** one mechanism for both cases. Each site is configured with its name, trust domain, token issuer, published key set URL, certificate bundle and per-service address, delivered through config state. This departs from the issue's "through the fleet's health state, across clusters", for two reasons:
  - There is no fleet-wide health state yet; each cluster's kernel keeps its own.
  - A pod in another cluster or deployment can't be reached anyway: nodes are private and NetworkPolicies are default-deny.

  Someone also has to own exposing each site's address, which isn't tacplus's job.
- **D3, trust:** federation of trust bundles rather than token exchange.
  - **Identity:** each identity stays in its own trust domain, and the far site can verify our tokens but never mint one.
  - **Dependencies:** no extra round trip to the far kernel on every connection.
  - **Precedent:** it has the same shape as Jody's 2026-10-29 kernel-to-kernel decision.
- **D4, tokens:** each token is bound to the receiving site, with the audience `remote:<site>:<svc>`. Without that, site B could replay site A's token into A's own local server. The server checks remote tokens with a separate verifier that requires:
  - the issuer is a configured site;
  - the subject is in that site's trust domain;
  - the audience names this site and service;
  - the caller is a service (not a workload or user) listed in `remote_auth_services`.
- **D5, kernel:** the kernel's token minter must allow those audiences. I asked permission to file a kernel issue, as #212 did with kernel#30.
- **D6, open questions:**
  - Is redo's second site in the same fleet? Since 2026-10-29 a fleet shares one trust domain. If the second site is in the same fleet, two things break: redo's receiver refuses its own trust domain, and it keys its logs by trust domain + tenant.
  - The flock-gossip driver looks superseded by the 2026-10-29 decisions, which dropped the flock service. I asked whether #206 should use this feature; I'd keep it out.
- **D7, tests:** the `cross-test/remote-s2s` fixture, covering the issue's admit and refuse cases plus the replay and wrong-issuer cases.
- **D8, scope:** the full job doesn't fit one 4h run. I proposed doing the contract and Rust here (redo is Rust), with Go and Java as follow-up issues required before the release, as #212 was split.

The issue stays open. Once Jody answers, the next run builds it test-first.
